::: {.doc-subject}

Compute the treewidth of a chordal graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L33663-L33744){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.chordal_graph_treewidth()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Chordal graph treewidth](../assets/graphs/chordal-graph-treewidth.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The same chordal graph: triangles A, B, C and B, C, D sharing an edge, plus a pendant E. Its largest cliques have three nodes; A, B, C is drawn in the accent color and B, C, D ties it. The card gives the treewidth as the largest clique size minus one, 3 − 1 = 2." width="100%"}

The treewidth measures how tree-like a graph is (see `tree_decomposition()`). It is
NP-hard to compute in general, but for a chordal graph it is simply the size of the
largest clique minus one, found in linear time. Use it to gauge the cost of inference
on a triangulated graphical model or of dynamic programming over a clique tree.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   The treewidth (`-1` for an empty graph).
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is directed or not chordal.
:::

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

The maximal cliques of `chordal_graph_cliques()` are exactly the bags of an optimal tree
decomposition, so the width is the largest clique size minus one. Matches NetworkX's
`chordal_graph_treewidth`.

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~chordal_graph_cliques`) 

:   List the maximal cliques of a chordal graph.

[](`~tree_decomposition`) 

:   A tree decomposition of any graph.

[](`~complete_to_chordal_graph`) 

:   Add edges to make a graph chordal.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

Two triangles sharing an edge, with a pendant node, have treewidth 2, one less than
their largest clique:

::: {#fbfed26e .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

graph = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "A"), ("B", "D"), ("C", "D"),
     ("D", "E")]
)
graph.chordal_graph_treewidth()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
2
```
:::
:::


### The cost of exact inference

`causal()` is a causal diagram of the risks for heart disease, with an arrow from each
cause to its effect. Its moral graph, which joins the parents of each variable, is
chordal with treewidth 4: Blood pressure and its four causes form a clique of five, so
exact inference works with tables over five variables at once. Leaving out the
unmeasured Genetics brings it down to 3:

::: {#efeeccd7 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
full = ec.causal()
known = ec.causal().delete_node("Genetics")

(
    full.moral_graph().chordal_graph_treewidth(),
    known.moral_graph().chordal_graph_treewidth(),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
(4, 3)
```
:::
:::


Without Genetics, the largest clique is Blood pressure with Age, Weight and Medication:

::: {#4b20b2aa .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from itertools import combinations

moral = known.moral_graph()
largest = max(moral.chordal_graph_cliques(), key=len)

moral.plot(
    layout="pos", highlight=list(combinations(largest, 2)),
    label_by="label", height=360,
)
```

